        "rows": [
          {
            "id": "h01",
            "answerable": true,
            "refused": false,
            "keyword_recall": 1,
            "citations": 4,
            "supported": 1,
            "invalid": 0,
            "verified": false,
            "ms": 1682,
            "answer": "According to Sun Tzu, the highest form of excellence in war is to break the enemy's resistance without fighting, which means to balk the enemy's plans and foil their intentions so that victory is achieved without shedding blood. This is considered superior to fighting and conquering in battle or destroying the enemy's forces [1][2][3][5]."
          },
          {
            "id": "h02",
            "answerable": true,
            "refused": false,
            "keyword_recall": 1,
            "citations": 2,
            "supported": 1,
            "invalid": 0,
            "verified": false,
            "ms": 1747,
            "answer": "The art of war is governed by five constant factors: (1) The Moral Law, (2) Heaven, (3) Earth, (4) The Commander, and (5) Method and discipline [1][4]."
          },
          {
            "id": "h03",
            "answerable": true,
            "refused": false,
            "keyword_recall": 1,
            "citations": 1,
            "supported": 1,
            "invalid": 0,
            "verified": true,
            "ms": 1259,
            "answer": "All warfare is based on deception [1]."
          },
          {
            "id": "h04",
            "answerable": true,
            "refused": false,
            "keyword_recall": 1,
            "citations": 2,
            "supported": 2,
            "invalid": 0,
            "verified": true,
            "ms": 1498,
            "answer": "If you know both the enemy and yourself, you need not fear the result of a hundred battles, implying assured success in warfare [1][2]."
          },
          {
            "id": "h05",
            "answerable": true,
            "refused": false,
            "keyword_recall": 0.2,
            "citations": 2,
            "supported": 2,
            "invalid": 0,
            "verified": true,
            "ms": 2482,
            "answer": "Sun Tzu describes five classes of spies, including \"converted spies,\" who are enemy spies captured and turned to work for one's own side by means of bribes and promises, using them to feed false information back to the enemy and spy on their own countrymen [4]. The text implies the use of other types of spies as well, but the specific five classes are not fully detailed in the provided excerpts. The importance of employing spies properly and paying them well is emphasized to obtain trustworthy intelligence [1]."
          },
          {
            "id": "h06",
            "answerable": true,
            "refused": false,
            "keyword_recall": 1,
            "citations": 2,
            "supported": 2,
            "invalid": 0,
            "verified": true,
            "ms": 1363,
            "answer": "The first way of attacking with fire is to burn soldiers in their camp [1][2]."
          },
          {
            "id": "h07",
            "answerable": true,
            "refused": false,
            "keyword_recall": 1,
            "citations": 1,
            "supported": 1,
            "invalid": 0,
            "verified": true,
            "ms": 2496,
            "answer": "The art of war recognizes nine varieties of ground: (1) Dispersive ground; (2) Facile ground; (3) Contentious ground; (4) Open ground; (5) Ground of intersecting highways; (6) Serious ground; (7) Difficult ground; (8) Hemmed-in ground; (9) Desperate ground [1]."
          },
          {
            "id": "h08",
            "answerable": true,
            "refused": false,
            "keyword_recall": 0,
            "citations": 1,
            "supported": 1,
            "invalid": 0,
            "verified": true,
            "ms": 1274,
            "answer": "No country has ever benefited from prolonged warfare, as stated explicitly in the text [1]."
          },
          {
            "id": "h09",
            "answerable": true,
            "refused": false,
            "keyword_recall": 1,
            "citations": 2,
            "supported": 2,
            "invalid": 0,
            "verified": true,
            "ms": 1606,
            "answer": "King Ho Lu of Wu took notice of Sun Tzu because of his book, the ART OF WAR [1][5]."
          },
          {
            "id": "h10",
