            "ms": 1738,
            "answer": "According to Epictetus, things within our power are opinion, aim, desire, aversion, and, in one word, whatever affairs are our own. These are by nature free, unrestricted, and unhindered [1]."
          },
          {
            "id": "h11",
            "answerable": true,
            "refused": false,
            "keyword_recall": 1,
            "citations": 2,
            "supported": 1,
            "invalid": 0,
            "verified": false,
            "ms": 1818,
            "answer": "According to the Enchiridion, people are disturbed not by things themselves, but by the views they take of those things. For example, death is not terrible in itself; rather, the terror comes from the notion that death is terrible. Therefore, disturbances arise from our own opinions and judgments, not from external events or actions by others [1][2]."
          },
          {
            "id": "h12",
            "answerable": true,
            "refused": false,
            "keyword_recall": 1,
            "citations": 1,
            "supported": 0,
            "invalid": 0,
            "verified": false,
            "ms": 1902,
            "answer": "Instead of saying \"I have lost it,\" you should say \"I have restored it.\" This applies to things like the death of a child, the death of a wife, or the loss of an estate, as these are seen as restorations rather than losses, since what was given can be demanded back by the giver at any time [1]."
          },
